ogged without its projection; operators\\nmust treat the event log as authority and repair the projection by replay.\\nA future runtime-owned transaction contract could coordinate the pair, but\\nthis provider must not emulate one with undocumented runtime internals.\\n=======\\nIf the projection step fails after a successful append, operators must treat the\\nevent log as authority and rebuild the projection. A future runtime-owned\\ntransaction contract could coordinate the pair, but this provider must not\\nemulate one with undocumented runtime internals.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/compatibility.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n| SurrealDB server `3.3.0` | Required stable lane | Pinned Docker image, remote WebSocket, server-side RocksDB |\\n| SurrealDB server `3.2.5` | Documented fallback | Re-pinned only if `3.3.0` fails conformance |\\n=======\\n| SurrealDB server `3.2.4` | Required stable lane | Pinned Docker image, remote WebSocket, server-side RocksDB |\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- [ActiveGraph PR #78](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/pull/78)\\n  proposed append-before-projection ordering and stronger run ownership. The\\n  released `1.12.0` baseline incorporates it: `Graph.emit` appends to the\\n  event store before projecting, and a failed append changes no graph,\\n  queue, projection, or observer state.\\n- [ActiveGraph issue #81](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/issues/81)\\n  tracks `Runtime.load()` creating an unknown run instead of failing without a\\n  mutation. It is re-evaluated against `1.12.0`; the strict test stays until\\n  the fix is proven.\\n- [ActiveGraph issue #82](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/issues/82)\\n  tracks replay into a stale supplied projection without clearing it first.\\n  It is re-evaluated against `1.12.0` the same way.\\n\\nThe ordering item is now a positive `1.12.0` contract test. The load and\\nstale-projection reproductions remain strict expected-failure tests until\\ntheir re-evaluation is proven. An upstream fix that makes one unexpectedly\\npass is a prompt to review and update the provider, not to delete the test\\nblindly.\\n\\n`1.12.0` also closes the patch vocabulary to `update` and `replace`:\\n`Patch` construction and replay both reject any other op with\\n`InvalidPatchOperation`. Provider-written stores are unaffected, because\\nevery stored patch passed framework validation at write time. A store\\nwritten by another `1.10`-era producer can hold `create` or `remove` ops\\n(which `1.10` accepted without validation and silently mis-applied); under\\n`1.12.0` such rows fail loudly at replay and at projection reads, with an\\nupstream error type rather than `SurrealProviderError`. That data requires\\nmigration or discard, not silent reads.\\n=======\\n- ActiveGraph `1.12.0` ships the append-before-projection ordering proposed by\\n  [PR #78](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/pull/78) as CONTRACT\\n  v1.11 #2: a rejected durable append changes no projected state. The provider\\n  asserts that invariant directly in `tests/test_write_ordering.py`.\\n- [ActiveGrap…[truncated 871 chars]","session_id":"ses_ecd1f104affewX9x4cIqy5fLvq","status":"completed"}}
