tus\":\"completed\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"=== git log for ADR files ===\\naa6f077 docs: record lane evidence and accept ADR 0003\\n1229dd4 Run the pack fixture suites on SurrealDB and match reference read order (#10)\\n266179a feat: enable the harness — ActiveGraph 1.12, SurrealQL pushdown, and the pack library on one store (#8)\\n09df653 docs: add upgrade plan and ADR 0003 draft\\n431d5d4 feat: add ActiveGraph SurrealDB provider preview\\n\\n=== 0004 head ===\\n# ADR 0004: Return projected objects in the reference store's insertion order\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-10-12\\n- Decision owners: TrendpilotAI\\n- Upstream baseline: ActiveGraph `v1.12.0` (`50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c`)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nActiveGraph's `GraphStore.all_objects` contract says only that it returns every\\nobject and that \\\"order is unspecified\\\". The reference `InMemoryGraphStore`\\nhappens to return objects in insertion order, and the runtime leans on that\\nobservable behavior in practice: object IDs come from one monotonic per-graph\\ncounter (`task#1`, `claim#2`, ...), and code such as the published pack\\nfixtures treats `graph.objects(type=...)[-1]` as \\\"the most recently created\\nobject of this type\\\".\\n\\nThe first SurrealDB projection ordered reads by `object_id ASC`. Because the\\nlogical IDs are strings, that is a lexicographic sort: it puts `comm_intent#10`\\nbefore `comm_intent#2`, and it orders across types by type name rather than by\\n\\n=== 0003-native-query-pushdown head ===\\n# ADR 0003: Push the graph query hooks into SurrealQL\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-10-12\\n- Decision owners: TrendpilotAI\\n- Upstream baseline: ActiveGraph `v1.12.0` (`50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c`)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nActiveGraph `1.12` added `GraphStore.query_objects` and kept the earlier\\noptional hooks (`find_objects`, `find_objects_in_types`, `find_relations`,\\n`neighborhood`, `match_chain`). Every hook has a working Python default that\\nscans `all_objects` / `all_relations`, so a backend that overrides nothing is\\nstill correct — it simply does all the work in the client process.\\n\\n\\n=== cross-refs to 0004 ===\\n./README.md:95:[ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md).\\n./docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md:1:# ADR 0004: Return projected objects in the reference store's insertion order\\n\"}],\"metadata\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit\":0}}","session_id":"ses_ecd1f104affewX9x4cIqy5fLvq","status":"completed"}}
