{"id":"int-9855046b2f1fa83cc928b990dd41046b","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:07:41.418Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"shell","extra":{"call_id":"call_6zXGhPGBYLvUcnvE9MMYn8C7","duration_ms":274,"input":"{\"command\":\"cd /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb && /work/.mercor-home/.local/bin/bd dolt commit --help 2>&1 | head -20 && echo \\\"---push---\\\" && /work/.mercor-home/.local/bin/bd dolt push --help 2>&1 | head -20\",\"timeout\":10000}","message_id":"msg_1333adc09001Bm7uF4qksK9unj","result":"{\"output\":{\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"output\":\"Create a Dolt commit from any uncommitted changes in the working set.\\n\\nThis is the primary commit point for batch mode. When auto-commit is set to\\n\\\"batch\\\", changes accumulate in the working set across multiple bd commands and\\nare committed together here with a descriptive summary message.\\n\\nAlso useful before push operations that require a clean working set, or when\\nauto-commit was off or changes were made externally.\\n\\nFor more options (--stdin, custom messages), see: bd vc commit\\n\\nUsage:\\n  bd dolt commit [flags]\\n\\nFlags:\\n  -h, --help             help for commit\\n  -m, --message string   Commit message (default: auto-generated)\\n\\nGlobal Flags:\\n      --actor string              Actor name for audit trail (default: $BEADS_ACTOR, git user.name, $USER)\\n---push---\\nPush local Dolt commits to the configured remote.\\n\\nRequires a Dolt remote to be configured in the database directory. With no\\nremote configured, bd can adopt one derived from git origin — only with\\nconsent: interactively, or via --yes; --no-adopt or BD_NO_REMOTE_ADOPT=1\\ndisables adoption entirely.\\nFor Hosted Dolt, set DOLT_REMOTE_USER and DOLT_REMOTE_PASSWORD environment\\nvariables for authentication.\\n\\nUse --force to overwrite remote changes (e.g., when the remote has\\nuncommitted changes in its working set).\\n\\nUse --remote to push to a specific named remote instead of the default.\\nThe remote must already exist (see 'bd dolt remote add').\\n\\nUsage:\\n  bd dolt push [flags]\\n\\nFlags:\\n      --force           Force push (overwrite remote changes)\\n\",\"status\":\"completed\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Create a Dolt commit from any uncommitted changes in the working set.\\n\\nThis is the primary commit point for batch mode. When auto-commit is set to\\n\\\"batch\\\", changes accumulate in the working set across multiple bd commands and\\nare committed together here with a descriptive summary message.\\n\\nAlso useful before push operations that require a clean working set, or when\\nauto-commit was off or changes were made externally.\\n\\nFor more options (--stdin, custom messages), see: bd vc commit\\n\\nUsage:\\n  bd dolt commit [flags]\\n\\nFlags:\\n  -h, --help             help for commit\\n  -m, --message string   Commit message (default: auto-generated)\\n\\nGlobal Flags:\\n      --actor string              Actor name for audit trail (default: $BEADS_ACTOR, git user.name, $USER)\\n---push---\\nPush local Dolt commits to the configured remote.\\n\\nRequires a Dolt remote to be configured in the database directory. With no\\nremote configured, bd can adopt one derived from git origin — only with\\nconsent: interactively, or via --yes; --no-adopt or BD_NO_REMOTE_ADOPT=1\\ndisables adoption entirely.\\nFor Hosted Dolt, set DOLT_REMOTE_USER and DOLT_REMOTE_PASSWORD environment\\nvariables for authentication.\\n\\nUse --force to overwrite remote changes (e.g., when the remote has\\nuncommitted changes in its working set).\\n\\nUse --remote to push to a specific named remote instead of the default.\\nThe remote must already exist (see 'bd dolt remote add').\\n\\nUsage:\\n  bd dolt push [flags]\\n\\nFlags:\\n      --force           Force push (overwrite remote changes)\\n\"}],\"metadata\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit\":0}}","session_id":"ses_eccd12d4cffe3Dd6d2N0q7gQQs","status":"completed"}}
