{"id":"int-1ee4d1c6fcc99aec04ae03ed3e32869a","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:22:54.166Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"read","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b74f8791f1012b12","duration_ms":21,"input":"{\"path\":\"/work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/security-model.md\"}","message_id":"msg_133489bd00015bEf51moRbVM7E","result":"{\"output\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"uri\":\"file:///work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/security-model.md\",\"name\":\"security-model.md\",\"content\":\"# Security model\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nThe provider protects the integrity and isolation of its own event and graph\\nrecords inside a configured SurrealDB namespace/database. It does not provide\\napplication authentication, customer authorization, secret management, network\\nperimeter controls, or policy for unrelated tables.\\n\\n## Assets and boundaries\\n\\nImportant assets are:\\n\\n- ordered event payloads and their run metadata;\\n- per-run sequence/head state;\\n- scope/run isolation;\\n- projection readiness state;\\n- database credentials and endpoints; and\\n- schema-version metadata.\\n\\nThe Python process and SurrealDB server are trusted to execute the provider\\ncorrectly. Callers are not trusted to provide identifier strings safe for query\\ninterpolation. Networks are untrusted unless separately protected; the local\\n`ws://` lane binds only to loopback and is not a remote-deployment pattern.\\n\\n## Required controls\\n\\n| Risk | Provider control |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Identifier/query injection | Opaque derived record keys; bound values and complete `RecordID` parameters; fixed provider table names |\\n| Cross-tenant or cross-run mutation | Every record and destructive operation is scoped by both scope and run |\\n| Concurrent append race | Event and head update share one SurrealDB transaction; stale expected heads fail closed |\\n| Partial replay served as current | Projection status changes to incomplete before clear and to ready only after successful replay |\\n| Silent event alteration | Canonical event material and previous hash are linked and verifiable |\\n| Credential disclosure | Secrets are environment-provided and must be absent from IDs, reprs, errors, events, and traces |\\n| Schema drift | Explicit version metadata; unknown versions fail closed |\\n\\nHash chaining is tamper evidence, not a digital signature, access-control system,\\nor defense against a database administrator who can rewrite both events and\\nhashes. Stronger adversaries require signed checkpoints or an independently\\nanchored digest, which is outside this preview.\\n\\n## Credentials\\n\\n- Never commit `.env` or real connection values.\\n- Use the example root identity only for an isolated local test server.\\n- Use a dedicated least-privilege identity and `wss://` for any remote evaluation.\\n- Do not include passwords or tokens in exceptions, object representations,\\n  record IDs, event payloads, traces, or CI artifacts.\\n- Rotate any credential that appears in a public log or issue; deleting the text\\n  is not sufficient remediation.\\n\\nThe initial configuration accepts `wss://` syntax but has no qualified TLS lane.\\nCertificate validation, proxies, workload identity, and rotation need explicit\\ndeployment tests before a remote security claim.\\n\\n## Non-atomic runtime boundary\\n\\nThe provider does not promise one transaction across event append and graph\\nprojection. A malicious input is not the only source of inconsistency; a normal\\nprocess or network failure between those operations can also diverge state.\\nMonitoring must detect failed writes and incomplete projections, and replay must\\nrepair from the authoritative event log.\\n\\n## Application responsibilities\\n\\nApplications sharing the database must separately define:\\n\\n- record/table permissions and identity mapping;\\n- encryption and key management;\\n- data classification, retention, deletion, and legal holds;\\n- source connector credentials and rate limits;\\n- audit access and redaction rules; and\\n- backup, restore, incident response, and high availability.\\n\\nProvider scope/run strings are isolation dimensions, not proof that an\\nauthenticated caller is authorized to use them.\\n\\nFor vulnerability reporting, follow [the repository security policy](../SECURITY.md).\\n\",\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"mime\":\"text/markdown\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read file /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/security-model.md, lines 1-79\\n1: # Security model\\n2: \\n3: ## Scope\\n4: \\n5: The provider protects the integrity and isolation of its own event and graph\\n6: records inside a configured SurrealDB namespace/database. It does not provide\\n7: application authentication, customer authorization, secret management, network\\n8: perimeter controls, or policy for unrelated tables.\\n9: \\n10: ## Assets and boundaries\\n11: \\n12: Important assets are:\\n13: \\n14: - ordered event payloads and their run metadata;\\n15: - per-run sequence/head state;\\n16: - scope/run isolation;\\n17: - projection readiness state;\\n18: - database credentials and endpoints; and\\n19: - schema-version metadata.\\n20: \\n21: The Python process and SurrealDB server are trusted to execute the provider\\n22: correctly. Callers are not trusted to provide identifier strings safe for query\\n23: interpolation. Networks are untrusted unless separately protected; the local\\n24: `ws://` lane binds only to loopback and is not a remote-deployment pattern.\\n25: \\n26: ## Required controls\\n27: \\n28: | Risk | Provider control |\\n29: | --- | --- |\\n30: | Identifier/query injection | Opaque derived record keys; bound values and complete `RecordID` parameters; fixed provider table names |\\n31: | Cross-tenant or cross-run mutation | Every record and destructive operation is scoped by both scope and run |\\n32: | Concurrent append race | Event and head update share one SurrealDB transaction; stale expected heads fail closed |\\n33: | Partial replay served as current | Projection status changes to incomplete before clear and to ready only after successful replay |\\n34: | Silent event alteration | Canonical event material and previous hash are linked and verifiable |\\n35: | Credential disclosure | Secrets are environment-provided and must be absent from IDs, reprs, errors, events, and traces |\\n36: | Schema drift | Explicit version metadata; unknown versions fail closed |\\n37: \\n38: Hash chaining is tamper evidence, not a digital signature, access-control system,\\n39: or defense against a database administrator who can rewrite both events and\\n40: hashes. Stronger adversaries require signed checkpoints or an independently\\n41: anchored digest, which is outside this preview.\\n42: \\n43: ## Credentials\\n44: \\n45: - Never commit `.env` or real connection values.\\n46: - Use the example root identity only for an isolated local test server.\\n47: - Use a dedicated least-privilege identity and `wss://` for any remote evaluation.\\n48: - Do not include passwords or tokens in exceptions, object representations,\\n49:   record IDs, event payloads, traces, or CI artifacts.\\n50: - Rotate any credential that appears in a public log or issue; deleting the text\\n51:   is not sufficient remediation.\\n52: \\n53: The initial configuration accepts `wss://` syntax but has no qualified TLS lane.\\n54: Certificate validation, proxies, workload identity, and rotation need explicit\\n55: deployment tests before a remote security claim.\\n56: \\n57: ## Non-atomic runtime boundary\\n58: \\n59: The provider does not promise one transaction across event append and graph\\n60: projection. A malicious input is not the only source of inconsistency; a normal\\n61: process or network failure between those operations can also diverge state.\\n62: Monitoring must detect failed writes and incomplete projections, and replay must\\n63: repair from the authoritative event log.\\n64: \\n65: ## Application responsibilities\\n66: \\n67: Applications sharing the database must separately define:\\n68: \\n69: - record/table permissions and identity mapping;\\n70: - encryption and key management;\\n71: - data classification, retention, deletion, and legal holds;\\n72: - source connector credentials and rate limits;\\n73: - audit access and redaction rules; and\\n74: - backup, restore, incident response, and high availability.\\n75: \\n76: Provider scope/run strings are isolation dimensions, not proof that an\\n77: authenticated caller is authorized to use them.\\n78: \\n79: For vulnerability reporting, follow [the repository security policy](../SECURITY.md).\"}],\"metadata\":{\"truncated\":false}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-bbd723d4e8e84ff9d32874cc6f2e598d","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:22:54.573Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"shell","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8292db2626076003","duration_ms":431,"input":"{\"command\":\"git log main --oneline -- docs/upgrade-plan.md | head -4; echo \\\"---branches containing it---\\\"; git branch -a --contains c22b62d 2>/dev/null | head -5; echo \\\"---compose pin---\\\"; grep -n 'image:\\\\|surrealdb/surrealdb' compose.yaml .github/workflows/*.yml 2>/dev/null | head -6; echo \\\"---adr 0003 history---\\\"; git log --oneline --all -- docs/adr/0003-native-query-pushdown.md docs/adr/0003-latest-stable-pins-and-exploration-lanes.md | head -5\"}","message_id":"msg_133489bd00015bEf51moRbVM7E","result":"{\"output\":{\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"output\":\"---branches containing it---\\n+ non-agentic/graph-db-upgrades\\n  remotes/origin/non-agentic/graph-db-upgrades\\n---compose pin---\\ncompose.yaml:5:    image: busybox:1.37.0\\ncompose.yaml:16:    image: surrealdb/surrealdb:v3.2.4\\n.github/workflows/qualification.yml:69:            surrealdb/surrealdb:v3.2.4 \\\\\\n---adr 0003 history---\\n2713b10 docs: record lane evidence and accept ADR 0003\\n8e1c86e feat: enable the harness — ActiveGraph 1.12, SurrealQL pushdown, and the pack library on one store (#8)\\nf281cd9 feat: load the ActiveGraph pack library on a SurrealDB event+graph store\\n09df653 docs: add upgrade plan and ADR 0003 draft\\n\",\"status\":\"completed\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"---branches containing it---\\n+ non-agentic/graph-db-upgrades\\n  remotes/origin/non-agentic/graph-db-upgrades\\n---compose pin---\\ncompose.yaml:5:    image: busybox:1.37.0\\ncompose.yaml:16:    image: surrealdb/surrealdb:v3.2.4\\n.github/workflows/qualification.yml:69:            surrealdb/surrealdb:v3.2.4 \\\\\\n---adr 0003 history---\\n2713b10 docs: record lane evidence and accept ADR 0003\\n8e1c86e feat: enable the harness — ActiveGraph 1.12, SurrealQL pushdown, and the pack library on one store (#8)\\nf281cd9 feat: load the ActiveGraph pack library on a SurrealDB event+graph store\\n09df653 docs: add upgrade plan and ADR 0003 draft\\n\"}],\"metadata\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit\":0}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-2bb85bc39e45f6b987b2cf9983a20b88","kind":"user_turn","created_at":"2026-10-13T03:22:55.526Z","actor":"opencode","prompt":"Yes, your approach is the supported mechanism — and the harness already uses it\nmodal-state.json shows how this sandbox was created:\n{ \"runtime\": \"vm\", \"snapshotId\": \"im-01M4RNJ3AHAWJT2V8F53EY77D4\", ... }\nim-… is a Modal filesystem-snapshot Image — so \"build an image with docker + restart the sandbox from it\" is the existing pipeline. The only change needed is to the image definition.\nBut it cannot be done from inside the sandbox — three independent reasons (all verified)\nBlocker\tEvidence\nAPI auth\tImage.build/publish, snapshot_filesystem(), Sandbox.create/from_id are Modal API calls → need the token (or the harness)\nBuilds run elsewhere\tDocs: \"Image builders run atop Modal's gVisor runtime\" — not inside this sandbox\nWe can't even stage docker in the root fs\t/usr/local/bin, /opt, /etc, /var/lib are root:root 755 → not writable. All our installs live under /work ($HOME → /work/.mercor-home)\nAnd one snapshot caveat that actually works in our favor\nDocs: \"Filesystem Snapshots only cover the Sandbox's root filesystem. Any mounted Volumes are not covered.\" — so a snapshot would not carry our tooling. That's fine: $HOME is on the /work volume, so a new sandbox mounting the same volume inherits everything (bd, the docker binaries, repos, .beads DB, transcripts) with zero rework.\n(Also relevant: runtime=\"vm\" + _experimental_enable_snapshot=True cannot mount Volumes, and memory snapshotting terminates the sandbox — so the right shape is filesystem-snapshot image + volume mount + dockerd entrypoint, not a memory snapshot.)\nThe exact delta for whoever owns the image\nimage = (\n    modal.Image.from_id(\"im-01M4RNJ3AHAWJT2V8F53EY77D4\")   # current base\n    .apt_install(\"docker.io\", \"docker-compose-v2\", \"iptables\", \"ca-certificates\")\n    .run_commands(\"mkdir -p /etc/docker && cat > /etc/docker/daemon.json <<'JSON'\\n\"\n                  '{ \"storage-driver\": \"overlay2\",\\n'\n                  '  \"registry-mirrors\": [\"https://mirror.gcr.io\"],\\n'\n                  '  \"bip\": \"10.200.0.1/24\",\\n'\n                  '  \"default-address-pools\": [{\"base\": \"10.201.0.0/16\", \"size\": 24}] }\\nJSON\")\n)\nsb = modal.Sandbox.create(\"dockerd\", image=image, runtime=\"vm\",\n                          volumes={\"/work\": modal.Volume.from_name(\"<the /work volume>\")},\n                          outbound_domain_allowlist=[\"*\"],      # or narrow at runtime\n                          readiness_probe=modal.Probe.with_exec(\"docker\", \"info\"))\nWhy each piece: dockerd as entrypoint (documented pattern); registry-mirrors because Docker Hub's S3 blob host isn't on the outbound allowlist (the networking doc explains: domain allowlists permit TLS only to listed domains); the 10.x relocation because Modal's control net is 172.16.0.0/12 and our eth0 is 172.20.21.143.\nMeanwhile — the ladder is now complete except the docker-blocked step\nThe harness status file shows the parallel session finished V6 (TLS), V7 (surrealkv), V8 (embedded), V9 (gates + docs, ADR 0003 accepted) on branch agentic/ladder-v6-v9 @ 2713b10, PR #12 open (not draft). Beads now has only two open items:\n- agvm-5am — V3 qualification (restart/kill) — hard-blocked on Docker\n- agvm-8ii — Docker provisioning (this conversation)\nSo docker is the only thing between the project and a fully closed ladder. Also, the transcript pipeline is now live and healthy (the single-file plugin registration I made was rejected — OpenCode 2 requires a directory package; it's been repackaged as ~/.config/opencode/plugins/beads-audit/index.ts): 211 tool calls, 88 llm calls, 20 user turns, 6 plugin starts recorded in .beads/interactions.jsonl.\nYour two options\n1. Paste the as-… secret → I run the updated create-docker-sandbox.py end-to-end myself and immediately execute V3.\n2. Hand Mercor the spec above → they add ~5 lines to the image definition; everything else (our tooling, beads, transcripts) comes along on the volume. ","extra":{"delivery":"steer","files":[],"session_id":"ses_ecd1f104affewX9x4cIqy5fLvq"}}
