{"id":"int-aea79a2bfe0f42edb10d17e1c7fd2ef3","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T16:14:50.978Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"shell","extra":{"call_id":"call_9f73807d1db143eaacdf4207","duration_ms":41,"input":"{\"command\":\"cd /home/mercor/orca/workspaces/activegraph-surrealdb/mercor-vm\\nfor f in pyproject.toml ROADMAP.md docs/experiment.md docs/deployment.md README.md docs/architecture.md docs/compatibility.md; do\\n  echo \\\"########## $f ##########\\\"\\n  awk 'BEGIN{c=0} /^<<<<<<< /{c++; print \\\"----- hunk \\\"c\\\" -----\\\"} c>0{print} /^>>>>>>> /{c=0; print \\\"\\\"}' \\\"$f\\\"\\ndone 2>&1 | head -230\",\"timeout\":120000}","message_id":"msg_1360b653f001KcgvfuHCSCQEtt","result":"{\"output\":{\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"output\":\"########## pyproject.toml ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n# ActiveGraph (through 1.12.0) exposes typed-looking classes without a PEP 561 marker.\\n=======\\n# ActiveGraph 1.12 exposes typed-looking classes without a PEP 561 marker.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## ROADMAP.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- [x] Re-evaluate append/projection ordering after an ActiveGraph release\\n  incorporates it; see\\n  [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-latest-stable-pins-and-exploration-lanes.md).\\n=======\\n- [x] Re-evaluate append/projection ordering after ActiveGraph 1.12 incorporated it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2).\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/experiment.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- behavior after an append or projection failure in ActiveGraph `1.12.0`;\\n=======\\n- behavior after an append failure in ActiveGraph `1.12.0`;\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/deployment.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n## Studio\\n\\nThe documented visual interface is SurrealDB Studio at\\n`studio.surrealdb.com`, delivered as a hosted web application and a\\ndesktop application. No UI image is pinned in the Compose deployment\\nbecause Studio is not self-hostable; Surrealist, now end-of-life, is not\\nadopted.\\n\\nThe desktop application is the clean path to the local Compose service:\\nconnect to `ws://127.0.0.1:8000` with the `SURREALDB_USER` and\\n`SURREALDB_PASSWORD` values from `.env`, and treat that root identity as\\nlocal-only. A hosted web page reaching a local endpoint is a verification\\nnote, not a claim: whether the hosted page can reach `ws://127.0.0.1:8000`\\nis not verified here, and an operator who needs that path must verify it\\nin their own environment before relying on it.\\n\\nStudio is an operator convenience. Qualification evidence comes from the\\ntest suites, never from a UI session.\\n=======\\n## Pack harness lane\\n\\nThe pack lane loads the external ActiveGraph pack library onto a Runtime whose\\nevent log and graph projection both live in the test database. Point the harness\\nat a checkout:\\n\\n```bash\\nACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH=/path/to/activegraph-packs pytest -m packs tests/packs\\n```\\n\\nWhen `ACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH` is unset the harness uses the reference checkout\\nbaked into this environment; the lane skips cleanly when neither is present. The\\nlane is read-only with respect to the pack repository — it imports packs and\\nloads them, and never modifies the checkout.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## README.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n| SurrealDB server | `3.3.0` stable |\\n=======\\n| SurrealDB server | `3.2.4` stable |\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\nappends its event before projecting it. A failed append changes nothing; a\\nfailed projection leaves an event logged without its projection, and replay\\nis the documented repair path. The provider does **not** claim atomic\\nevent-plus-projection acceptance.\\n=======\\npersists its event before projecting it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2), so a rejected\\nappend leaves no projected state. The projection is still a separate write: if\\nit fails after a successful append, replay is the documented repair path and the\\nprovider does **not** claim atomic event-plus-projection acceptance.\\n\\n## Taking advantage of one database\\n\\nBecause the event log and the graph projection share one deployment, the\\nprovider pushes the released `GraphStore` query hooks down into SurrealQL rather\\nthan scanning in Python. Object type filters, relation source/target/type\\nfilters, existence probes, and the undirected neighbourhood walk are all bound\\nSurrealQL; `match_chain` composes those pushed-down primitives hop by hop. The\\nrich `where` predicate stays with ActiveGraph and is returned as an exact\\nresidual, per the released contract. Schema v2 adds the indexes those queries\\nuse; schema v3 records the object counter so reads return the reference store's\\ninsertion order. See [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-native-query-pushdown.md) and\\n[ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md).\\n\\n## Pack library\\n\\nThe provider ships a harness that loads the published\\n[ActiveGraph pack library](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph-packs)\\nonto a `Runtime` whose event log and graph projection both live in SurrealDB:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom activegraph_surrealdb import SurrealConfig, build_runtime\\n\\nhandle = build_runtime(\\n    SurrealConfig(endpoint=\\\"ws://127.0.0.1:8000\\\", namespace=\\\"activegraph\\\",\\n                  database=\\\"assistant\\\", username=\\\"root\\\", password=\\\"root\\\"),\\n    run_id=\\\"run_1\\\",\\n    scope=\\\"assistant\\\",\\n)\\nhandle.runtime.graph.add_object(\\\"source\\\", {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"note\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"...\\\"})\\nhandle.runtime.run_until_idle()\\nhandle.close()\\n```\\n\\nPoint the harness at a checkout with `ACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH` (default: the\\nreference checkout). The `packs` test lane proves core, infrastructure,\\ncommunication (including composio), bridges, mcp, attention, evolution,\\nsemantic_extraction, eval_outcome, activity_normalizer, connector_control,\\nskills, usage, and the importers all load and project into one SurrealDB\\ndatabase.\\n\\nA second lane runs each pack's own `fixtures/run_fixtures.py` unchanged on\\nSurrealDB-backed stores (`tests/packs/test_pack_fixtures_on_surrealdb.py`). All\\nbut two suites pass; `communication` reads an object it created after a behavior\\npatched it, which only works with the in-memory object graph, and `evolution`\\ndrives SQLite-backed subprocess forks. Both are recorded as expected divergences\\nrather than hidden.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/architecture.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\nActiveGraph `1.12.0` appends a live event through `EventStore.append()`\\nbefore projecting it into the graph. The two provider stores have\\nindependent SurrealDB transaction handles, and the runtime does not expose\\na transaction coordinator spanning them. Therefore:\\n=======\\nActiveGraph `1.12.0` persists a live event before projecting it (CONTRACT\\nv1.11 #2), so a rejected append changes no projected state. The two provider\\nstores still have independent SurrealDB transaction handles, and the runtime\\ndoes not expose a transaction coordinator spanning them. Therefore:\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\nA failed append changes no graph, queue, projection, or observer state. A\\nfailed projection leaves an event logged without its projection; operators\\nmust treat the event log as authority and repair the projection by replay.\\nA future runtime-owned transaction contract could coordinate the pair, but\\nthis provider must not emulate one with undocumented runtime internals.\\n=======\\nIf the projection step fails after a successful append, operators must treat the\\nevent log as authority and rebuild the projection. A future runtime-owned\\ntransaction contract could coordinate the pair, but this provider must not\\nemulate one with undocumented runtime internals.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/compatibility.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n| SurrealDB server `3.3.0` | Required stable lane | Pinned Docker image, remote WebSocket, server-side RocksDB |\\n| SurrealDB server `3.2.5` | Documented fallback | Re-pinned only if `3.3.0` fails conformance |\\n=======\\n| SurrealDB server `3.2.4` | Required stable lane | Pinned Docker image, remote WebSocket, server-side RocksDB |\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- [ActiveGraph PR #78](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/pull/78)\\n  proposed append-before-projection ordering and stronger run ownership. The\\n  released `1.12.0` baseline incorporates it: `Graph.emit` appends to the\\n  event store before projecting, and a failed append changes no graph,\\n  queue, projection, or observer state.\\n- [ActiveGraph issue #81](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/issues/81)\\n  tracks `Runtime.load()` creating an unknown run instead of failing without a\\n  mutation. It is re-evaluated against `1.12.0`; the strict test stays until\\n  the fix is proven.\\n- [ActiveGraph issue #82](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/issues/82)\\n  tracks replay into a stale supplied projection without clearing it first.\\n  It is re-evaluated against `1.12.0` the same way.\\n\\nThe ordering item is now a positive `1.12.0` contract test. The load and\\nstale-projection reproductions remain strict expected-failure tests until\\ntheir re-evaluation is proven. An upstream fix that makes one unexpectedly\\npass is a prompt to review and update the provider, not to delete the test\\nblindly.\\n\\n`1.12.0` also closes the patch vocabulary to `update` and `replace`:\\n`Patch` construction and replay both reject any other op with\\n`InvalidPatchOperation`. Provider-written stores are unaffected, because\\nevery stored patch passed framework validation at write time. A store\\nwritten by another `1.10`-era producer can hold `create` or `remove` ops\\n(which `1.10` accepted without validation and silently mis-applied); under\\n`1.12.0` such rows fail loudly at replay and at projection reads, with an\\nupstream error type rather than `SurrealProviderError`. That data requires\\nmigration or discard, not silent reads.\\n=======\\n- ActiveGraph `1.12.0` ships the append-before-projection ordering proposed by\\n  [PR #78](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/pull/78) as CONTRACT\\n  v1.11 #2: a rejected durable append changes no projected state. The provider\\n  asserts that invariant directly in `tests/test_write_ordering.py`.\\n- [ActiveGraph issue #81](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/issues/81)\\n  tracks `Runtime.load()` creating an unknown run instead of failing without a\\n  mutation. Still open in `1.12.0`.\\n- [ActiveGraph issue #82](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph/issues/82)\\n  tracks replay into a stale supplied projection without clearing it first.\\n  Still open in `1.12.0`.\\n\\nStrict expected-failure tests keep the remaining limitations visible. An\\nupstream fix that makes one unexpectedly pass is a prompt to review and update\\nthe provider, not to delete the test blindly.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- ActiveGraph compatibility beyond the pinned `1.12.0` baseline.\\n=======\\n- ActiveGraph `1.13` compatibility before an upstream release is tested.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n\",\"status\":\"completed\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"########## pyproject.toml ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n# ActiveGraph (through 1.12.0) exposes typed-looking classes without a PEP 561 marker.\\n=======\\n# ActiveGraph 1.12 exposes typed-looking classes without a PEP 561 marker.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## ROADMAP.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- [x] Re-evaluate append/projection ordering after an ActiveGraph release\\n  incorporates it; see\\n  [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-latest-stable-pins-and-exploration-lanes.md).\\n=======\\n- [x] Re-evaluate append/projection ordering after ActiveGraph 1.12 incorporated it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2).\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/experiment.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n- behavior after an append or projection failure in ActiveGraph `1.12.0`;\\n=======\\n- behavior after an append failure in ActiveGraph `1.12.0`;\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## docs/deployment.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n## Studio\\n\\nThe documented visual interface is SurrealDB Studio at\\n`studio.surrealdb.com`, delivered as a hosted web application and a\\ndesktop application. No UI image is pinned in the Compose deployment\\nbecause Studio is not self-hostable; Surrealist, now end-of-life, is not\\nadopted.\\n\\nThe desktop application is the clean path to the local Compose service:\\nconnect to `ws://127.0.0.1:8000` with the `SURREALDB_USER` and\\n`SURREALDB_PASSWORD` values from `.env`, and treat that root identity as\\nlocal-only. A hosted web page reaching a local endpoint is a verification\\nnote, not a claim: whether the hosted page can reach `ws://127.0.0.1:8000`\\nis not verified here, and an operator who needs that path must verify it\\nin their own environment before relying on it.\\n\\nStudio is an operator convenience. Qualification evidence comes from the\\ntest suites, never from a UI session.\\n=======\\n## Pack harness lane\\n\\nThe pack lane loads the external ActiveGraph pack library onto a Runtime whose\\nevent log and graph projection both live in the test database. Point the harness\\nat a checkout:\\n\\n```bash\\nACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH=/path/to/activegraph-packs pytest -m packs tests/packs\\n```\\n\\nWhen `ACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH` is unset the harness uses the reference checkout\\nbaked into this environment; the lane skips cleanly when neither is present. The\\nlane is read-only with respect to the pack repository — it imports packs and\\nloads them, and never modifies the checkout.\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n########## README.md ##########\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\n| SurrealDB server | `3.3.0` stable |\\n=======\\n| SurrealDB server | `3.2.4` stable |\\n>>>>>>> origin/main\\n\\n----- hunk 1 -----\\n<<<<<<< HEAD\\nappends its event before projecting it. A failed append changes nothing; a\\nfailed projection leaves an event logged without its projection, and replay\\nis the documented repair path. The provider does **not** claim atomic\\nevent-plus-projection acceptance.\\n=======\\npersists its event before projecting it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2), so a rejected\\nappend leaves no projected state. The projection is still a separate write: if\\nit fails after a successful append, replay is the documented repair path and the\\nprovider does **not** claim atomic event-plus-projection acceptance.\\n\\n## Taking advantage of one database\\n\\nBecause the event log and the graph projection share one deployment, the\\nprovider pushes the released `GraphStore` query hooks down into SurrealQL rather\\nthan scanning in Python. Object type filters, relation source/target/type\\nfilters, existence probes, and the undirected neighbourhood walk are all bound\\nSurrealQL; `match_chain` composes those pushed-down primitives hop by hop. The\\nrich `where` predicate stays with ActiveGraph and is returned as an exact\\nresidual, per the released contract. Schema v2 adds the indexes those queries\\nuse; schema v3 records the object counter so reads return the reference store's\\ninsertion order. See [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-native-query-pushdown.md) and\\n[ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md).\\n\\n## Pack library\\n\\nThe provider ships a harness that loads the published\\n[ActiveGraph pack library](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph-packs)\\nonto a `Runtime` whose event log and graph projection both live in SurrealDB:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom activegraph_surrealdb import SurrealConfig, build_runtime\\n\\nhandle = build_runtime(\\n    SurrealConfig(endpoint=\\\"ws://1