{"id":"int-392221a4da31886fdcb5c02cd3b0cbed","kind":"user_turn","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:38:37.909Z","actor":"opencode","prompt":"Give me a simple explanation of what ActiveGraph is and what the SurrealDB adaptor does and what the 3 workstreams enable 1. Upgrading the database and ensuring conformance testing 2. implementing Telemetry and traces for evals 3. Creating an agentic harness ","extra":{"delivery":"steer","files":[],"session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p"}}
{"id":"int-d91da18128befd5abecda7ea1c0be4ef","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:38:57.298Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"read","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a02119dad4c20584","duration_ms":7,"input":"{\"path\":\"/work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/README.md\"}","message_id":"msg_1376af1fc001GvS1vYb4i8O9tY","result":"{\"output\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"uri\":\"file:///work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/README.md\",\"name\":\"README.md\",\"content\":\"# activegraph-surrealdb\\n\\n`activegraph-surrealdb` is a research-preview provider that explores using one\\nSurrealDB deployment for two distinct ActiveGraph responsibilities:\\n\\n- an authoritative, append-ordered event log; and\\n- a queryable graph projection that can be erased and rebuilt from that log.\\n\\nThe important idea is not “put everything in one table.” It is “operate one\\ndatabase without confusing the record of what happened with a disposable view\\nof what the world looks like now.”\\n\\n> **Status: research preview.** The repository uses contract-first development.\\n> Passing tests are evidence for the tested versions and failure paths only;\\n> they are not a production-readiness, high-availability, backup, performance,\\n> or security certification.\\n\\n## Why a reactive, event-sourced graph?\\n\\nA mutable graph is excellent for questions such as “what is connected to this\\nrecord now?” It is a poor audit trail by itself: replacing an object can erase\\nhow the system reached that answer. An event log preserves the decisions and\\ninputs in order, but replaying the whole log for every query is expensive.\\n\\nKeeping both gives a useful recovery and experimentation loop:\\n\\n1. append facts and decisions to the event log;\\n2. project them into a graph for current-state queries;\\n3. copy a run prefix to fork an alternative decision;\\n4. diff the two outcomes; and\\n5. delete and replay a projection to prove it is derived state.\\n\\nSee [the experiment guide](docs/experiment.md) for the executable trace and the\\nlimits of what it proves.\\n\\n## Decision matrix\\n\\n| Option | Durable events | Shared graph reads | Native graph edges | Operational shape | Fit here |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| SurrealDB over WebSocket with RocksDB | Provider goal | Provider goal | Yes | One single-node deployment in the qualified lane | Chosen research path |\\n| PostgreSQL plus a separate graph database | Mature event-store path | Yes | In the graph service | Two databases and a synchronization boundary | Strong alternative when operational maturity matters more than consolidation |\\n| SQLite plus an in-process graph | Local durable events | Process-local | No shared native graph | Very small | Development and upstream reproduction |\\n| Embedded SurrealDB or SurrealKV | Potentially | Potentially | Yes | In-process | Not qualified by this preview |\\n\\nThis project does not claim that SurrealDB is a universal replacement for\\nPostgreSQL or a distributed graph service. It evaluates a bounded provider\\ncontract against a pinned, repeatable configuration.\\n\\n## Supported research lane\\n\\n| Component | Baseline |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| ActiveGraph | `1.12.0` |\\n| SurrealDB server | `3.2.4` stable |\\n| SurrealDB Python SDK | `2.0.0` |\\n| Python | `3.11+` |\\n| Transport | remote WebSocket; local qualification uses `ws://` |\\n| Storage engine | server-side RocksDB |\\n\\nSurrealDB `3.3.x` prereleases are a non-gating preview lane. HTTP, embedded\\nengines, and TLS qualification are not part of the initial evidence. Details\\nare in [compatibility and known issues](docs/compatibility.md).\\n\\n## Architecture at a glance\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    A[ActiveGraph runtime] -->|append ordered event| E[SurrealEventStore]\\n    A -->|project current state| G[SurrealGraphStore]\\n    E --> EL[(SurrealDB event tables)]\\n    G --> GP[(SurrealDB object, patch, and relation tables)]\\n    EL -->|clear and replay| G\\n    EL -->|copy prefix| F[Forked run]\\n    F -->|diff outcomes| A\\n```\\n\\nThe two stores may share a namespace and database, but they do not share an\\nActiveGraph-owned transaction. On ActiveGraph `1.12.0`, a runtime operation\\npersists its event before projecting it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2), so a rejected\\nappend leaves no projected state. The projection is still a separate write: if\\nit fails after a successful append, replay is the documented repair path and the\\nprovider does **not** claim atomic event-plus-projection acceptance.\\n\\n## Taking advantage of one database\\n\\nBecause the event log and the graph projection share one deployment, the\\nprovider pushes the released `GraphStore` query hooks down into SurrealQL rather\\nthan scanning in Python. Object type filters, relation source/target/type\\nfilters, existence probes, and the undirected neighbourhood walk are all bound\\nSurrealQL; `match_chain` composes those pushed-down primitives hop by hop. The\\nrich `where` predicate stays with ActiveGraph and is returned as an exact\\nresidual, per the released contract. Schema v2 adds the indexes those queries\\nuse; schema v3 records the object counter so reads return the reference store's\\ninsertion order. See [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-native-query-pushdown.md) and\\n[ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md).\\n\\n## Pack library\\n\\nThe provider ships a harness that loads the published\\n[ActiveGraph pack library](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph-packs)\\nonto a `Runtime` whose event log and graph projection both live in SurrealDB:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom activegraph_surrealdb import SurrealConfig, build_runtime\\n\\nhandle = build_runtime(\\n    SurrealConfig(\\n        endpoint=\\\"ws://127.0.0.1:8000\\\",\\n        namespace=\\\"activegraph\\\",\\n        database=\\\"assistant\\\",\\n        username=\\\"root\\\",\\n        password=\\\"root\\\",\\n    ),\\n    run_id=\\\"run_1\\\",\\n    scope=\\\"assistant\\\",\\n)\\nhandle.runtime.graph.add_object(\\\"source\\\", {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"note\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"...\\\"})\\nhandle.runtime.run_until_idle()\\nhandle.close()\\n```\\n\\nPoint the harness at a checkout with `ACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH` (default: the\\nreference checkout). The `packs` test lane proves core, infrastructure,\\ncommunication (including composio), bridges, mcp, attention, evolution,\\nsemantic_extraction, eval_outcome, activity_normalizer, connector_control,\\nskills, usage, and the importers all load and project into one SurrealDB\\ndatabase.\\n\\nA second lane runs each pack's own `fixtures/run_fixtures.py` unchanged on\\nSurrealDB-backed stores (`tests/packs/test_pack_fixtures_on_surrealdb.py`). All\\nbut two suites pass; `communication` reads an object it created after a behavior\\npatched it, which only works with the in-memory object graph, and `evolution`\\ndrives SQLite-backed subprocess forks. Both are recorded as expected divergences\\nrather than hidden.\\n\\nRead the full [architecture](docs/architecture.md) and [security model](docs/security-model.md).\\n\\n## Appropriate use cases\\n\\n- auditable agent or workflow state where alternative decisions need comparison;\\n- reconciliation systems that need a current graph plus an ordered evidence log;\\n- identity or entity-resolution experiments with reviewable forks;\\n- derived relationship projections that must survive process restarts; and\\n- evaluating SurrealDB as a consolidated operational data plane.\\n\\nDo not use this preview as the sole basis for a production system that requires\\ndistributed high availability, proven backup/restore, unqualified transports,\\nor atomic writes across the event and projection stores.\\n\\nApplication contacts, identities, credentials, source documents, and CRM data\\nare outside this provider's schema. An application may choose the same\\nSurrealDB deployment for them, but it must design its own authorization,\\nretention, and migration boundaries.\\n\\n## Quick start\\n\\nPrerequisites: Python `3.11+`, Docker with Compose, and a local port `8000`.\\n\\n```bash\\ncp .env.example .env\\n# Replace both example password values in .env with the same local-only value.\\nset -a\\n. ./.env\\nset +a\\n\\ndocker compose up -d\\npython -m venv .venv\\n. .venv/bin/activate\\npython -m pip install -e '.[dev]'\\n```\\n\\nRun the contract layers separately:\\n\\n```bash\\npytest -m 'not surrealdb and not qualification'\\npytest -m surrealdb tests/integration\\npytest -m packs tests/packs            # pack harness + per-pack fixture suites\\npython examples/replay_fork_diff.py\\n```\\n\\nThe qualification suite deliberately restarts and kills the named container.\\nRun it only against the disposable Compose service:\\n\\n```bash\\nACTIVEGRAPH_SURREALDB_QUALIFY=1 pytest -m qualification tests/qualification\\n```\\n\\nSee [deployment](docs/deployment.md) and [conformance](docs/conformance.md) before\\ninterpreting a green result.\\n\\n## Project map\\n\\n- `src/activegraph_surrealdb/`: provider implementation\\n- `src/activegraph_surrealdb/harness.py`: load the pack library onto a SurrealDB Runtime\\n- `tests/`: unit, live integration, qualification, pack-harness and per-pack fixture, and upstream-known-issue contracts\\n- `examples/replay_fork_diff.py`: small fork/diff/replay experiment\\n- `examples/combined_store_pack.py`: a pack running on one SurrealDB event+graph store\\n- `docs/adr/`: authority, version, pushdown, and projection-order decisions\\n- `docs/`: architecture, operations, evidence, security, and provenance\\n\\n## Contributing and security\\n\\nRead [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) before proposing a change. Report\\nvulnerabilities through the private process in [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a\\npublic issue.\\n\\n## License\\n\\nCopyright 2026 TrendpilotAI. Licensed under the [Apache License 2.0](LICENSE).\\nDependency and trademark notes are recorded in [NOTICE](NOTICE).\\n\",\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"mime\":\"text/markdown\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read file /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/README.md, lines 1-210\\n1: # activegraph-surrealdb\\n2: \\n3: `activegraph-surrealdb` is a research-preview provider that explores using one\\n4: SurrealDB deployment for two distinct ActiveGraph responsibilities:\\n5: \\n6: - an authoritative, append-ordered event log; and\\n7: - a queryable graph projection that can be erased and rebuilt from that log.\\n8: \\n9: The important idea is not “put everything in one table.” It is “operate one\\n10: database without confusing the record of what happened with a disposable view\\n11: of what the world looks like now.”\\n12: \\n13: > **Status: research preview.** The repository uses contract-first development.\\n14: > Passing tests are evidence for the tested versions and failure paths only;\\n15: > they are not a production-readiness, high-availability, backup, performance,\\n16: > or security certification.\\n17: \\n18: ## Why a reactive, event-sourced graph?\\n19: \\n20: A mutable graph is excellent for questions such as “what is connected to this\\n21: record now?” It is a poor audit trail by itself: replacing an object can erase\\n22: how the system reached that answer. An event log preserves the decisions and\\n23: inputs in order, but replaying the whole log for every query is expensive.\\n24: \\n25: Keeping both gives a useful recovery and experimentation loop:\\n26: \\n27: 1. append facts and decisions to the event log;\\n28: 2. project them into a graph for current-state queries;\\n29: 3. copy a run prefix to fork an alternative decision;\\n30: 4. diff the two outcomes; and\\n31: 5. delete and replay a projection to prove it is derived state.\\n32: \\n33: See [the experiment guide](docs/experiment.md) for the executable trace and the\\n34: limits of what it proves.\\n35: \\n36: ## Decision matrix\\n37: \\n38: | Option | Durable events | Shared graph reads | Native graph edges | Operational shape | Fit here |\\n39: | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n40: | SurrealDB over WebSocket with RocksDB | Provider goal | Provider goal | Yes | One single-node deployment in the qualified lane | Chosen research path |\\n41: | PostgreSQL plus a separate graph database | Mature event-store path | Yes | In the graph service | Two databases and a synchronization boundary | Strong alternative when operational maturity matters more than consolidation |\\n42: | SQLite plus an in-process graph | Local durable events | Process-local | No shared native graph | Very small | Development and upstream reproduction |\\n43: | Embedded SurrealDB or SurrealKV | Potentially | Potentially | Yes | In-process | Not qualified by this preview |\\n44: \\n45: This project does not claim that SurrealDB is a universal replacement for\\n46: PostgreSQL or a distributed graph service. It evaluates a bounded provider\\n47: contract against a pinned, repeatable configuration.\\n48: \\n49: ## Supported research lane\\n50: \\n51: | Component | Baseline |\\n52: | --- | --- |\\n53: | ActiveGraph | `1.12.0` |\\n54: | SurrealDB server | `3.2.4` stable |\\n55: | SurrealDB Python SDK | `2.0.0` |\\n56: | Python | `3.11+` |\\n57: | Transport | remote WebSocket; local qualification uses `ws://` |\\n58: | Storage engine | server-side RocksDB |\\n59: \\n60: SurrealDB `3.3.x` prereleases are a non-gating preview lane. HTTP, embedded\\n61: engines, and TLS qualification are not part of the initial evidence. Details\\n62: are in [compatibility and known issues](docs/compatibility.md).\\n63: \\n64: ## Architecture at a glance\\n65: \\n66: ```mermaid\\n67: flowchart LR\\n68:     A[ActiveGraph runtime] -->|append ordered event| E[SurrealEventStore]\\n69:     A -->|project current state| G[SurrealGraphStore]\\n70:     E --> EL[(SurrealDB event tables)]\\n71:     G --> GP[(SurrealDB object, patch, and relation tables)]\\n72:     EL -->|clear and replay| G\\n73:     EL -->|copy prefix| F[Forked run]\\n74:     F -->|diff outcomes| A\\n75: ```\\n76: \\n77: The two stores may share a namespace and database, but they do not share an\\n78: ActiveGraph-owned transaction. On ActiveGraph `1.12.0`, a runtime operation\\n79: persists its event before projecting it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2), so a rejected\\n80: append leaves no projected state. The projection is still a separate write: if\\n81: it fails after a successful append, replay is the documented repair path and the\\n82: provider does **not** claim atomic event-plus-projection acceptance.\\n83: \\n84: ## Taking advantage of one database\\n85: \\n86: Because the event log and the graph projection share one deployment, the\\n87: provider pushes the released `GraphStore` query hooks down into SurrealQL rather\\n88: than scanning in Python. Object type filters, relation source/target/type\\n89: filters, existence probes, and the undirected neighbourhood walk are all bound\\n90: SurrealQL; `match_chain` composes those pushed-down primitives hop by hop. The\\n91: rich `where` predicate stays with ActiveGraph and is returned as an exact\\n92: residual, per the released contract. Schema v2 adds the indexes those queries\\n93: use; schema v3 records the object counter so reads return the reference store's\\n94: insertion order. See [ADR 0003](docs/adr/0003-native-query-pushdown.md) and\\n95: [ADR 0004](docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md).\\n96: \\n97: ## Pack library\\n98: \\n99: The provider ships a harness that loads the published\\n100: [ActiveGraph pack library](https://github.com/yoheinakajima/activegraph-packs)\\n101: onto a `Runtime` whose event log and graph projection both live in SurrealDB:\\n102: \\n103: ```python\\n104: from activegraph_surrealdb import SurrealConfig, build_runtime\\n105: \\n106: handle = build_runtime(\\n107:     SurrealConfig(\\n108:         endpoint=\\\"ws://127.0.0.1:8000\\\",\\n109:         namespace=\\\"activegraph\\\",\\n110:         database=\\\"assistant\\\",\\n111:         username=\\\"root\\\",\\n112:         password=\\\"root\\\",\\n113:     ),\\n114:     run_id=\\\"run_1\\\",\\n115:     scope=\\\"assistant\\\",\\n116: )\\n117: handle.runtime.graph.add_object(\\\"source\\\", {\\\"kind\\\": \\\"note\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"...\\\"})\\n118: handle.runtime.run_until_idle()\\n119: handle.close()\\n120: ```\\n121: \\n122: Point the harness at a checkout with `ACTIVEGRAPH_PACKS_PATH` (default: the\\n123: reference checkout). The `packs` test lane proves core, infrastructure,\\n124: communication (including composio), bridges, mcp, attention, evolution,\\n125: semantic_extraction, eval_outcome, activity_normalizer, connector_control,\\n126: skills, usage, and the importers all load and project into one SurrealDB\\n127: database.\\n128: \\n129: A second lane runs each pack's own `fixtures/run_fixtures.py` unchanged on\\n130: SurrealDB-backed stores (`tests/packs/test_pack_fixtures_on_surrealdb.py`). All\\n131: but two suites pass; `communication` reads an object it created after a behavior\\n132: patched it, which only works with the in-memory object graph, and `evolution`\\n133: drives SQLite-backed subprocess forks. Both are recorded as expected divergences\\n134: rather than hidden.\\n135: \\n136: Read the full [architecture](docs/architecture.md) and [security model](docs/security-model.md).\\n137: \\n138: ## Appropriate use cases\\n139: \\n140: - auditable agent or workflow state where alternative decisions need comparison;\\n141: - reconciliation systems that need a current graph plus an ordered evidence log;\\n142: - identity or entity-resolution experiments with reviewable forks;\\n143: - derived relationship projections that must survive process restarts; and\\n144: - evaluating SurrealDB as a consolidated operational data plane.\\n145: \\n146: Do not use this preview as the sole basis for a production system that requires\\n147: distributed high availability, proven backup/restore, unqualified transports,\\n148: or atomic writes across the event and projection stores.\\n149: \\n150: Application contacts, identities, credentials, source documents, and CRM data\\n151: are outside this provider's schema. An application may choose the same\\n152: SurrealDB deployment for them, but it must design its own authorization,\\n153: retention, and migration boundaries.\\n154: \\n155: ## Quick start\\n156: \\n157: Prerequisites: Python `3.11+`, Docker with Compose, and a local port `8000`.\\n158: \\n159: ```bash\\n160: cp .env.example .env\\n161: # Replace both example password values in .env with the same local-only value.\\n162: set -a\\n163: . ./.env\\n164: set +a\\n165: \\n166: docker compose up -d\\n167: python -m venv .venv\\n168: . .venv/bin/activate\\n169: python -m pip install -e '.[dev]'\\n170: ```\\n171: \\n172: Run the contract layers separately:\\n173: \\n174: ```bash\\n175: pytest -m 'not surrealdb and not qualification'\\n176: pytest -m surrealdb tests/integration\\n177: pytest -m packs tests/packs            # pack harness + per-pack fixture suites\\n178: python examples/replay_fork_diff.py\\n179: ```\\n180: \\n181: The qualification suite deliberately restarts and kills the named container.\\n182: Run it only against the disposable Compose service:\\n183: \\n184: ```bash\\n185: ACTIVEGRAPH_SURREALDB_QUALIFY=1 pytest -m qualification tests/qualification\\n186: ```\\n187: \\n188: See [deployment](docs/deployment.md) and [conformance](docs/conformance.md) before\\n189: interpreting a green result.\\n190: \\n191: ## Project map\\n192: \\n193: - `src/activegraph_surrealdb/`: provider implementation\\n194: - `src/activegraph_surrealdb/harness.py`: load the pack library onto a SurrealDB Runtime\\n195: - `tests/`: unit, live integration, qualification, pack-harness and per-pack fixture, and upstream-known-issue contracts\\n196: - `examples/replay_fork_diff.py`: small fork/diff/replay experiment\\n197: - `examples/combined_store_pack.py`: a pack running on one SurrealDB event+graph store\\n198: - `docs/adr/`: authority, version, pushdown, and projection-order decisions\\n199: - `docs/`: architecture, operations, evidence, security, and provenance\\n200: \\n201: ## Contributing and security\\n202: \\n203: Read [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) before proposing a change. Report\\n204: vulnerabilities through the private process in [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md), not a\\n205: public issue.\\n206: \\n207: ## License\\n208: \\n209: Copyright 2026 TrendpilotAI. Licensed under the [Apache License 2.0](LICENSE).\\n210: Dependency and trademark notes are recorded in [NOTICE](NOTICE).\"}],\"metadata\":{\"truncated\":false}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-018f85839375cdee9b5f2266b23ad11b","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:38:57.317Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"read","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-894ee0308968df63","duration_ms":26,"input":"{\"path\":\"/work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/ROADMAP.md\"}","message_id":"msg_1376af1fc001GvS1vYb4i8O9tY","result":"{\"output\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"uri\":\"file:///work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/ROADMAP.md\",\"name\":\"ROADMAP.md\",\"content\":\"# Roadmap\\n\\nThis roadmap describes evidence gates, not release dates. Work moves forward\\nonly when the previous boundary is repeatably demonstrated.\\n\\n## Phase 0 — Contract-first research preview\\n\\n- [x] Separate authoritative event and disposable graph responsibilities.\\n- [x] Pin ActiveGraph `1.12.0`, SurrealDB `3.2.4`, and Python SDK `2.0.0` lanes.\\n- [x] Define serialization, isolation, replay, fork/diff, and qualification contracts.\\n- [x] Record upstream lifecycle limitations without private runtime patches.\\n\\n## Phase 1 — Generic provider implementation\\n\\n- [x] Pass unit serialization/configuration contracts.\\n- [x] Pass released EventStore and GraphStore conformance.\\n- [x] Implement transactional append/head checks and deterministic stale-writer tests.\\n- [x] Implement native relation storage without an in-memory mirror.\\n- [x] Push object, relation, existence, and neighbourhood queries into native SurrealQL; `match_chain` composes those primitives.\\n- [x] Implement fail-closed replay readiness and uncompacted prefix forks.\\n- [x] Load the published pack library onto one SurrealDB-backed Runtime.\\n- [x] Publish reproducible fork/diff/replay trace evidence.\\n\\n## Phase 2 — Stable qualification\\n\\n- [x] Pass live isolation, rollback, native-edge, and exact-JSON tests.\\n- [ ] Pass pinned RocksDB graceful restart and abrupt container termination tests.\\n- [x] Add an in-place, data-preserving schema upgrade lane and reject a newer schema.\\n- [x] Add a schema rollback lane before changing support.\\n- [x] Exercise backup and clean-environment restore, then verify event integrity and replay.\\n- [ ] Record bounded performance observations on controlled, representative data.\\n\\n## Phase 3 — Upstream lifecycle alignment\\n\\n- [x] Re-evaluate append/projection ordering after ActiveGraph 1.12 incorporated it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2).\\n- [ ] Adopt a public store-driver/registry contract if upstream accepts one.\\n- [ ] Adopt store-neutral load/resume without phantom-run or stale-projection behavior.\\n- [ ] Design snapshot/compaction hydration only against an explicit upstream contract.\\n- [ ] Evaluate an optional runtime-owned cross-store transaction coordinator.\\n\\n## Phase 4 — Production evaluation\\n\\n- [ ] Qualify `wss://`, certificate validation, least-privilege identities, and rotation.\\n- [ ] Define monitoring and operator response for failed/incomplete projections.\\n- [ ] Document data retention, restore objectives, and incident response.\\n- [ ] Decide whether single-node RocksDB meets availability requirements or a different\\n  deployment architecture is necessary.\\n- [ ] Complete an independent security and operational review.\\n\\n## Continuing non-goals\\n\\n- claiming cross-store atomicity without an upstream coordinator;\\n- hiding upstream gaps behind copied private runtime code;\\n- managing application contacts, auth, CRM records, or identity ontologies;\\n- qualifying every SurrealDB transport, engine, cloud, or prerelease; and\\n- calling the preview production-ready based only on CI.\\n\",\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"mime\":\"text/markdown\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read file /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/ROADMAP.md, lines 1-56\\n1: # Roadmap\\n2: \\n3: This roadmap describes evidence gates, not release dates. Work moves forward\\n4: only when the previous boundary is repeatably demonstrated.\\n5: \\n6: ## Phase 0 — Contract-first research preview\\n7: \\n8: - [x] Separate authoritative event and disposable graph responsibilities.\\n9: - [x] Pin ActiveGraph `1.12.0`, SurrealDB `3.2.4`, and Python SDK `2.0.0` lanes.\\n10: - [x] Define serialization, isolation, replay, fork/diff, and qualification contracts.\\n11: - [x] Record upstream lifecycle limitations without private runtime patches.\\n12: \\n13: ## Phase 1 — Generic provider implementation\\n14: \\n15: - [x] Pass unit serialization/configuration contracts.\\n16: - [x] Pass released EventStore and GraphStore conformance.\\n17: - [x] Implement transactional append/head checks and deterministic stale-writer tests.\\n18: - [x] Implement native relation storage without an in-memory mirror.\\n19: - [x] Push object, relation, existence, and neighbourhood queries into native SurrealQL; `match_chain` composes those primitives.\\n20: - [x] Implement fail-closed replay readiness and uncompacted prefix forks.\\n21: - [x] Load the published pack library onto one SurrealDB-backed Runtime.\\n22: - [x] Publish reproducible fork/diff/replay trace evidence.\\n23: \\n24: ## Phase 2 — Stable qualification\\n25: \\n26: - [x] Pass live isolation, rollback, native-edge, and exact-JSON tests.\\n27: - [ ] Pass pinned RocksDB graceful restart and abrupt container termination tests.\\n28: - [x] Add an in-place, data-preserving schema upgrade lane and reject a newer schema.\\n29: - [x] Add a schema rollback lane before changing support.\\n30: - [x] Exercise backup and clean-environment restore, then verify event integrity and replay.\\n31: - [ ] Record bounded performance observations on controlled, representative data.\\n32: \\n33: ## Phase 3 — Upstream lifecycle alignment\\n34: \\n35: - [x] Re-evaluate append/projection ordering after ActiveGraph 1.12 incorporated it (CONTRACT v1.11 #2).\\n36: - [ ] Adopt a public store-driver/registry contract if upstream accepts one.\\n37: - [ ] Adopt store-neutral load/resume without phantom-run or stale-projection behavior.\\n38: - [ ] Design snapshot/compaction hydration only against an explicit upstream contract.\\n39: - [ ] Evaluate an optional runtime-owned cross-store transaction coordinator.\\n40: \\n41: ## Phase 4 — Production evaluation\\n42: \\n43: - [ ] Qualify `wss://`, certificate validation, least-privilege identities, and rotation.\\n44: - [ ] Define monitoring and operator response for failed/incomplete projections.\\n45: - [ ] Document data retention, restore objectives, and incident response.\\n46: - [ ] Decide whether single-node RocksDB meets availability requirements or a different\\n47:   deployment architecture is necessary.\\n48: - [ ] Complete an independent security and operational review.\\n49: \\n50: ## Continuing non-goals\\n51: \\n52: - claiming cross-store atomicity without an upstream coordinator;\\n53: - hiding upstream gaps behind copied private runtime code;\\n54: - managing application contacts, auth, CRM records, or identity ontologies;\\n55: - qualifying every SurrealDB transport, engine, cloud, or prerelease; and\\n56: - calling the preview production-ready based only on CI.\"}],\"metadata\":{\"truncated\":false}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-14e138205337067859f8b5e0b339c5d3","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:38:59.466Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"read","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-84514200825305c8","duration_ms":5,"input":"{\"path\":\"/work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/architecture.md\"}","message_id":"msg_1376b3dbd001XqLJpcCYeC8hql","result":"{\"output\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"uri\":\"file:///work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/architecture.md\",\"name\":\"architecture.md\",\"content\":\"# Architecture\\n\\n## The authority boundary\\n\\nThe event store and graph store answer different questions:\\n\\n- **Event store:** What happened, in what order, and in which run?\\n- **Graph store:** What objects, relations, and proposed patches are visible now?\\n\\nThe event log is authoritative. The graph is a materialized projection. Sharing\\none SurrealDB namespace/database reduces operational surface area, but it does\\nnot merge these responsibilities or make every write atomic.\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart TB\\n    subgraph Runtime[ActiveGraph runtime]\\n      C[Command or observation]\\n      D[Diff and inspection]\\n    end\\n\\n    subgraph SurrealDB[One SurrealDB namespace/database]\\n      R[(Run metadata and heads)]\\n      E[(Ordered events and hashes)]\\n      O[(Projected objects and patches)]\\n      V[(Vertex records)]\\n      X[(TYPE RELATION edges)]\\n    end\\n\\n    C --> E\\n    C --> O\\n    C --> X\\n    E --> R\\n    E -->|replay| O\\n    E -->|replay| X\\n    E -->|copy prefix| FE[(Fork event log)]\\n    FE -->|replay| FG[(Fork projection)]\\n    O --> D\\n    FG --> D\\n    X --> V\\n```\\n\\n## Provider seams\\n\\n`SurrealEventStore` is scoped by both `scope` and `run_id`. Its contract requires:\\n\\n- monotonically ordered, gap-free sequence positions;\\n- run-scoped logical event uniqueness;\\n- a per-run head written in the same SurrealDB transaction as each append;\\n- stale-writer detection rather than silent rebasing;\\n- exact canonical JSON for replay and a hash linked to the previous event; and\\n- prefix truncation and copy operations that preserve the resulting head.\\n\\n`SurrealGraphStore` uses the same scope/run boundary for objects, patches,\\nvertices, and relations. Relations are planned as native `TYPE RELATION` records\\nwith SurrealDB `RecordID` values in `in` and `out`. Placeholder vertices allow\\nActiveGraph's valid dangling-edge behavior without inventing graph objects.\\n\\nTable names beginning with `ag_` are owned by the provider. Application tables\\nare not managed, migrated, cleared, replayed, or authorized by this package.\\n\\n## Record identity\\n\\nLogical identifiers can include characters that are unsafe to interpolate into\\nSurrealQL. The provider therefore derives opaque record keys from the record\\nkind, scope, run, and logical ID. Logical values remain fields for round-trip\\nreconstruction. Queries bind values and complete `RecordID` instances as\\nparameters; user-controlled table and relation names are not accepted.\\n\\nThe same logical event or object ID may exist in a different scope or run. A\\nclear, truncate, fork, or replay operation must never cross that compound\\nboundary.\\n\\n## Query pushdown\\n\\nThe released `GraphStore` exposes optional query hooks that default to Python\\nscans over `all_objects` / `all_relations`. `SurrealGraphStore` overrides them so\\nthe structural work runs in SurrealDB:\\n\\n- `query_objects` consumes the object type, applies an optional limit when no\\n  residual predicate remains, and answers `result_mode=\\\"exists\\\"` with a\\n  `LIMIT 1` probe. It returns the plan's `where` unchanged as the residual, so\\n  ActiveGraph keeps ownership of the rich predicate language.\\n- `find_objects` / `find_objects_in_types` / `find_relations` filter by type,\\n  source, and target in bound SurrealQL.\\n- `neighborhood` walks the frontier one level at a time, querying only the\\n  relations incident to the current frontier. This mirrors the base class's\\n  level-by-level semantics exactly (placeholders and cycles included) without\\n  loading unreachable relations.\\n- `match_chain` is inherited from the base class and therefore composes the\\n  pushed-down primitives hop by hop rather than scanning the whole graph.\\n\\nSchema v2 adds the `(scope, run_id, ...)` indexes these queries use. Schema v3\\nadds `ag_vertex.object_seq`, which records the framework's object counter so\\nreads return objects in the reference in-memory store's insertion order rather\\nthan a lexicographic sort of the logical IDs. The pushdown is pinned by\\n`tests/integration/test_graph_pushdown.py`, which disables the `all_objects` /\\n`all_relations` fallback and then exercises every hook.\\n\\n## Live writes are not cross-store atomic\\n\\nActiveGraph `1.12.0` persists a live event before projecting it (CONTRACT\\nv1.11 #2), so a rejected append changes no projected state. The two provider\\nstores still have independent SurrealDB transaction handles, and the runtime\\ndoes not expose a transaction coordinator spanning them. Therefore:\\n\\n- an event-store append is atomic with its own run head;\\n- a graph mutation is atomic within its own store operation; but\\n- the pair is not advertised as one atomic acceptance transaction.\\n\\nIf the projection step fails after a successful append, operators must treat the\\nevent log as authority and rebuild the projection. A future runtime-owned\\ntransaction contract could coordinate the pair, but this provider must not\\nemulate one with undocumented runtime internals.\\n\\n## Replay lifecycle\\n\\nReplay is destructive only inside the target provider scope/run projection:\\n\\n1. mark the target projection incomplete;\\n2. clear its objects, patches, vertices, and relations;\\n3. apply the event history in order; and\\n4. mark the target ready only after the final event succeeds.\\n\\nA failed replay remains failed/incomplete, and normal reads must fail closed\\nrather than serve a half-built graph. The contract rejects compacted logs whose\\nfirst retained event is `runtime.snapshot`; snapshot hydration and retained\\nhorizon semantics belong to a future upstream lifecycle contract.\\n\\n## Fork and diff\\n\\nA fork copies an event prefix into a new run in the same scope and records its\\nparent run and fork event. Parent and child then accept independent suffixes.\\nEach run receives its own projection. ActiveGraph's diff surface can compare the\\nshared prefix, unique events, and divergent objects without rewriting the\\nparent's history.\\n\\nThe helper is a provider operation, not a replacement for full\\n`Runtime.load()`/resume. Behaviors, approvals, caches, queued work, and snapshot\\nstate remain outside the helper's contract.\\n\",\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"mime\":\"text/markdown\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read file /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/architecture.md, lines 1-139\\n1: # Architecture\\n2: \\n3: ## The authority boundary\\n4: \\n5: The event store and graph store answer different questions:\\n6: \\n7: - **Event store:** What happened, in what order, and in which run?\\n8: - **Graph store:** What objects, relations, and proposed patches are visible now?\\n9: \\n10: The event log is authoritative. The graph is a materialized projection. Sharing\\n11: one SurrealDB namespace/database reduces operational surface area, but it does\\n12: not merge these responsibilities or make every write atomic.\\n13: \\n14: ```mermaid\\n15: flowchart TB\\n16:     subgraph Runtime[ActiveGraph runtime]\\n17:       C[Command or observation]\\n18:       D[Diff and inspection]\\n19:     end\\n20: \\n21:     subgraph SurrealDB[One SurrealDB namespace/database]\\n22:       R[(Run metadata and heads)]\\n23:       E[(Ordered events and hashes)]\\n24:       O[(Projected objects and patches)]\\n25:       V[(Vertex records)]\\n26:       X[(TYPE RELATION edges)]\\n27:     end\\n28: \\n29:     C --> E\\n30:     C --> O\\n31:     C --> X\\n32:     E --> R\\n33:     E -->|replay| O\\n34:     E -->|replay| X\\n35:     E -->|copy prefix| FE[(Fork event log)]\\n36:     FE -->|replay| FG[(Fork projection)]\\n37:     O --> D\\n38:     FG --> D\\n39:     X --> V\\n40: ```\\n41: \\n42: ## Provider seams\\n43: \\n44: `SurrealEventStore` is scoped by both `scope` and `run_id`. Its contract requires:\\n45: \\n46: - monotonically ordered, gap-free sequence positions;\\n47: - run-scoped logical event uniqueness;\\n48: - a per-run head written in the same SurrealDB transaction as each append;\\n49: - stale-writer detection rather than silent rebasing;\\n50: - exact canonical JSON for replay and a hash linked to the previous event; and\\n51: - prefix truncation and copy operations that preserve the resulting head.\\n52: \\n53: `SurrealGraphStore` uses the same scope/run boundary for objects, patches,\\n54: vertices, and relations. Relations are planned as native `TYPE RELATION` records\\n55: with SurrealDB `RecordID` values in `in` and `out`. Placeholder vertices allow\\n56: ActiveGraph's valid dangling-edge behavior without inventing graph objects.\\n57: \\n58: Table names beginning with `ag_` are owned by the provider. Application tables\\n59: are not managed, migrated, cleared, replayed, or authorized by this package.\\n60: \\n61: ## Record identity\\n62: \\n63: Logical identifiers can include characters that are unsafe to interpolate into\\n64: SurrealQL. The provider therefore derives opaque record keys from the record\\n65: kind, scope, run, and logical ID. Logical values remain fields for round-trip\\n66: reconstruction. Queries bind values and complete `RecordID` instances as\\n67: parameters; user-controlled table and relation names are not accepted.\\n68: \\n69: The same logical event or object ID may exist in a different scope or run. A\\n70: clear, truncate, fork, or replay operation must never cross that compound\\n71: boundary.\\n72: \\n73: ## Query pushdown\\n74: \\n75: The released `GraphStore` exposes optional query hooks that default to Python\\n76: scans over `all_objects` / `all_relations`. `SurrealGraphStore` overrides them so\\n77: the structural work runs in SurrealDB:\\n78: \\n79: - `query_objects` consumes the object type, applies an optional limit when no\\n80:   residual predicate remains, and answers `result_mode=\\\"exists\\\"` with a\\n81:   `LIMIT 1` probe. It returns the plan's `where` unchanged as the residual, so\\n82:   ActiveGraph keeps ownership of the rich predicate language.\\n83: - `find_objects` / `find_objects_in_types` / `find_relations` filter by type,\\n84:   source, and target in bound SurrealQL.\\n85: - `neighborhood` walks the frontier one level at a time, querying only the\\n86:   relations incident to the current frontier. This mirrors the base class's\\n87:   level-by-level semantics exactly (placeholders and cycles included) without\\n88:   loading unreachable relations.\\n89: - `match_chain` is inherited from the base class and therefore composes the\\n90:   pushed-down primitives hop by hop rather than scanning the whole graph.\\n91: \\n92: Schema v2 adds the `(scope, run_id, ...)` indexes these queries use. Schema v3\\n93: adds `ag_vertex.object_seq`, which records the framework's object counter so\\n94: reads return objects in the reference in-memory store's insertion order rather\\n95: than a lexicographic sort of the logical IDs. The pushdown is pinned by\\n96: `tests/integration/test_graph_pushdown.py`, which disables the `all_objects` /\\n97: `all_relations` fallback and then exercises every hook.\\n98: \\n99: ## Live writes are not cross-store atomic\\n100: \\n101: ActiveGraph `1.12.0` persists a live event before projecting it (CONTRACT\\n102: v1.11 #2), so a rejected append changes no projected state. The two provider\\n103: stores still have independent SurrealDB transaction handles, and the runtime\\n104: does not expose a transaction coordinator spanning them. Therefore:\\n105: \\n106: - an event-store append is atomic with its own run head;\\n107: - a graph mutation is atomic within its own store operation; but\\n108: - the pair is not advertised as one atomic acceptance transaction.\\n109: \\n110: If the projection step fails after a successful append, operators must treat the\\n111: event log as authority and rebuild the projection. A future runtime-owned\\n112: transaction contract could coordinate the pair, but this provider must not\\n113: emulate one with undocumented runtime internals.\\n114: \\n115: ## Replay lifecycle\\n116: \\n117: Replay is destructive only inside the target provider scope/run projection:\\n118: \\n119: 1. mark the target projection incomplete;\\n120: 2. clear its objects, patches, vertices, and relations;\\n121: 3. apply the event history in order; and\\n122: 4. mark the target ready only after the final event succeeds.\\n123: \\n124: A failed replay remains failed/incomplete, and normal reads must fail closed\\n125: rather than serve a half-built graph. The contract rejects compacted logs whose\\n126: first retained event is `runtime.snapshot`; snapshot hydration and retained\\n127: horizon semantics belong to a future upstream lifecycle contract.\\n128: \\n129: ## Fork and diff\\n130: \\n131: A fork copies an event prefix into a new run in the same scope and records its\\n132: parent run and fork event. Parent and child then accept independent suffixes.\\n133: Each run receives its own projection. ActiveGraph's diff surface can compare the\\n134: shared prefix, unique events, and divergent objects without rewriting the\\n135: parent's history.\\n136: \\n137: The helper is a provider operation, not a replacement for full\\n138: `Runtime.load()`/resume. Behaviors, approvals, caches, queued work, and snapshot\\n139: state remain outside the helper's contract.\"}],\"metadata\":{\"truncated\":false}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-f5b6240e3baa38fcb8bc60ac4191c5aa","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:38:59.472Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"read","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af950042314bd527","duration_ms":10,"input":"{\"path\":\"/work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/experiment.md\"}","message_id":"msg_1376b3dbd001XqLJpcCYeC8hql","result":"{\"output\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"uri\":\"file:///work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/experiment.md\",\"name\":\"experiment.md\",\"content\":\"# Fork, diff, and replay experiment\\n\\n## Question\\n\\nWhy keep an authoritative event log when SurrealDB can already store and query a\\ngraph?\\n\\nThe experiment creates one decision run, forks it at a shared event, makes two\\ndifferent decisions, diffs the outcomes, deliberately contaminates one graph\\nprojection with a ghost object, and rebuilds it from events.\\n\\nIf the event log is authority and the projection is derived, the trace must show:\\n\\n1. parent and child share the same event prefix;\\n2. each has a distinct suffix and divergent current state;\\n3. the parent event history is unchanged by the fork;\\n4. replay reproduces the parent's pre-contamination graph exactly; and\\n5. the ghost disappears because it was never in the event history.\\n\\n## Run it\\n\\nStart the pinned environment and export `.env` as described in\\n[deployment.md](deployment.md), then run:\\n\\n```bash\\npython examples/replay_fork_diff.py\\n```\\n\\nThe script exits non-zero if a required invariant is false. Its JSON output has\\nthis shape; IDs and hashes are generated at runtime:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"event_log\\\": {\\n    \\\"event_count\\\": 2,\\n    \\\"head_hash\\\": \\\"<sha256>\\\",\\n    \\\"next_sequence\\\": 2\\n  },\\n  \\\"fork\\\": {\\n    \\\"divergent_object_ids\\\": [\\\"<object-id>\\\"],\\n    \\\"fork_only_event_ids\\\": [\\\"<event-id>\\\"],\\n    \\\"parent_only_event_ids\\\": [\\\"<event-id>\\\"],\\n    \\\"shared_event_ids\\\": [\\\"<event-id>\\\"]\\n  },\\n  \\\"projection\\\": {\\n    \\\"ghost_removed\\\": true,\\n    \\\"rebuilt_exactly\\\": true,\\n    \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"scope\\\": \\\"experiment_<random>\\\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## What the trace proves\\n\\nFor the pinned server, SDK, package commit, and successful execution, the trace\\nis direct evidence that:\\n\\n- a fork is a prefix copy rather than an edit to the parent;\\n- alternative suffixes produce a meaningful ActiveGraph diff;\\n- event records survive independently of a corrupted/stale graph row; and\\n- replay clears and deterministically reconstructs the provider projection.\\n\\nThat is the practical value of an event-sourced reactive graph: operators can\\nrecover a query model and investigators can compare alternative decisions\\nwithout destroying the original sequence.\\n\\nActiveGraph's deterministic ID generator may assign the same event ID to the\\nfirst divergent event in both runs. That is valid because event identity is\\nscoped by run. The experiment therefore compares complete events and projected\\nstate, not event-ID strings alone, when proving that a parent-only suffix did\\nnot leak into the fork.\\n\\n## What it does not prove\\n\\nThe experiment does not prove:\\n\\n- atomic event-plus-projection acceptance;\\n- behavior after an append failure in ActiveGraph `1.12.0`;\\n- snapshot/compaction or full `Runtime.load()` resume;\\n- TLS, authorization, backup/restore, distributed availability, or upgrades;\\n- representative performance or capacity; or\\n- correctness for application schemas outside the provider.\\n\\nUse the broader [conformance](conformance.md) and qualification suites for their\\nspecific claims, and keep the promotion gates in the ADRs visible.\\n\",\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"mime\":\"text/markdown\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read file /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/experiment.md, lines 1-86\\n1: # Fork, diff, and replay experiment\\n2: \\n3: ## Question\\n4: \\n5: Why keep an authoritative event log when SurrealDB can already store and query a\\n6: graph?\\n7: \\n8: The experiment creates one decision run, forks it at a shared event, makes two\\n9: different decisions, diffs the outcomes, deliberately contaminates one graph\\n10: projection with a ghost object, and rebuilds it from events.\\n11: \\n12: If the event log is authority and the projection is derived, the trace must show:\\n13: \\n14: 1. parent and child share the same event prefix;\\n15: 2. each has a distinct suffix and divergent current state;\\n16: 3. the parent event history is unchanged by the fork;\\n17: 4. replay reproduces the parent's pre-contamination graph exactly; and\\n18: 5. the ghost disappears because it was never in the event history.\\n19: \\n20: ## Run it\\n21: \\n22: Start the pinned environment and export `.env` as described in\\n23: [deployment.md](deployment.md), then run:\\n24: \\n25: ```bash\\n26: python examples/replay_fork_diff.py\\n27: ```\\n28: \\n29: The script exits non-zero if a required invariant is false. Its JSON output has\\n30: this shape; IDs and hashes are generated at runtime:\\n31: \\n32: ```json\\n33: {\\n34:   \\\"event_log\\\": {\\n35:     \\\"event_count\\\": 2,\\n36:     \\\"head_hash\\\": \\\"<sha256>\\\",\\n37:     \\\"next_sequence\\\": 2\\n38:   },\\n39:   \\\"fork\\\": {\\n40:     \\\"divergent_object_ids\\\": [\\\"<object-id>\\\"],\\n41:     \\\"fork_only_event_ids\\\": [\\\"<event-id>\\\"],\\n42:     \\\"parent_only_event_ids\\\": [\\\"<event-id>\\\"],\\n43:     \\\"shared_event_ids\\\": [\\\"<event-id>\\\"]\\n44:   },\\n45:   \\\"projection\\\": {\\n46:     \\\"ghost_removed\\\": true,\\n47:     \\\"rebuilt_exactly\\\": true,\\n48:     \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\"\\n49:   },\\n50:   \\\"scope\\\": \\\"experiment_<random>\\\"\\n51: }\\n52: ```\\n53: \\n54: ## What the trace proves\\n55: \\n56: For the pinned server, SDK, package commit, and successful execution, the trace\\n57: is direct evidence that:\\n58: \\n59: - a fork is a prefix copy rather than an edit to the parent;\\n60: - alternative suffixes produce a meaningful ActiveGraph diff;\\n61: - event records survive independently of a corrupted/stale graph row; and\\n62: - replay clears and deterministically reconstructs the provider projection.\\n63: \\n64: That is the practical value of an event-sourced reactive graph: operators can\\n65: recover a query model and investigators can compare alternative decisions\\n66: without destroying the original sequence.\\n67: \\n68: ActiveGraph's deterministic ID generator may assign the same event ID to the\\n69: first divergent event in both runs. That is valid because event identity is\\n70: scoped by run. The experiment therefore compares complete events and projected\\n71: state, not event-ID strings alone, when proving that a parent-only suffix did\\n72: not leak into the fork.\\n73: \\n74: ## What it does not prove\\n75: \\n76: The experiment does not prove:\\n77: \\n78: - atomic event-plus-projection acceptance;\\n79: - behavior after an append failure in ActiveGraph `1.12.0`;\\n80: - snapshot/compaction or full `Runtime.load()` resume;\\n81: - TLS, authorization, backup/restore, distributed availability, or upgrades;\\n82: - representative performance or capacity; or\\n83: - correctness for application schemas outside the provider.\\n84: \\n85: Use the broader [conformance](conformance.md) and qualification suites for their\\n86: specific claims, and keep the promotion gates in the ADRs visible.\"}],\"metadata\":{\"truncated\":false}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-fa55948cfbcbeb34b6d48f9d704a97b4","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:39:01.948Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"read","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4fd8dc423fa97d5","duration_ms":51,"input":"{\"path\":\"/work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/provenance.md\"}","message_id":"msg_1376b4639001jO4o4qsOcM70Cs","result":"{\"output\":{\"type\":\"file\",\"uri\":\"file:///work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/provenance.md\",\"name\":\"provenance.md\",\"content\":\"# Provenance and publication boundary\\n\\nThis repository is intended to be a generic ActiveGraph provider. Public source,\\ntests, examples, and documentation must be reviewable without access to any\\napplication repository, customer system, private contact record, or internal\\ncredential.\\n\\n## Inputs to the design\\n\\n- public ActiveGraph `1.12.0` interfaces and conformance suites;\\n- public ActiveGraph issues and pull requests cited in the known-issue tests;\\n- public SurrealDB `3.2.4` and Python SDK `2.0.0` documentation/interfaces; and\\n- generic, synthetic examples created for this provider.\\n\\nThe repository imports ActiveGraph and the SurrealDB Python SDK as external\\ndependencies. It does not vendor their source. Dependency packages, names, and\\ntrademarks retain their own licenses and ownership; see [NOTICE](../NOTICE).\\n\\n## Excluded material\\n\\nDo not contribute:\\n\\n- application-specific adapters, schemas, names, paths, or architecture notes;\\n- customer, contact, CRM, mailbox, device, or identity data;\\n- production endpoints, account IDs, tokens, passwords, or environment files;\\n- generated bytecode, caches, database files, traces containing payloads, or\\n  local absolute paths; or\\n- code copied from a source whose license or contributor authority is unclear.\\n\\nSynthetic fixtures should be obviously fictional or historical and contain no\\nreal email address, phone number, account identifier, or source-system key.\\n\\n## Contribution provenance\\n\\nBy submitting a contribution, authors must have the right to license it under\\nApache-2.0. Reimplement behavior from public contracts and documentation; do not\\npaste private application code and merely rename symbols. If a change adapts\\nlicensed upstream material, identify the source, version, license, and material\\nchanges in the pull request and preserve any required notices.\\n\\nKnown-issue reproductions link their public upstream report. Those tests are\\nevidence of an upstream lifecycle boundary, not a claim that this provider owns\\nor has fixed ActiveGraph itself.\\n\\n## Pre-publication check\\n\\nBefore every public commit or release:\\n\\n1. inspect the exact staged diff, including binary and untracked files;\\n2. scan source, docs, examples, fixtures, and built artifacts for secrets,\\n   personal data, application names, and local paths;\\n3. confirm generated files and `.env` remain ignored;\\n4. verify license headers/notices for any adapted material;\\n5. build the sdist and wheel and inspect their complete file lists; and\\n6. record the commit and test evidence without embedding private payloads.\\n\",\"encoding\":\"utf8\",\"mime\":\"text/markdown\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read file /work/mercor-work/activegraph-surrealdb/docs/provenance.md, lines 1-55\\n1: # Provenance and publication boundary\\n2: \\n3: This repository is intended to be a generic ActiveGraph provider. Public source,\\n4: tests, examples, and documentation must be reviewable without access to any\\n5: application repository, customer system, private contact record, or internal\\n6: credential.\\n7: \\n8: ## Inputs to the design\\n9: \\n10: - public ActiveGraph `1.12.0` interfaces and conformance suites;\\n11: - public ActiveGraph issues and pull requests cited in the known-issue tests;\\n12: - public SurrealDB `3.2.4` and Python SDK `2.0.0` documentation/interfaces; and\\n13: - generic, synthetic examples created for this provider.\\n14: \\n15: The repository imports ActiveGraph and the SurrealDB Python SDK as external\\n16: dependencies. It does not vendor their source. Dependency packages, names, and\\n17: trademarks retain their own licenses and ownership; see [NOTICE](../NOTICE).\\n18: \\n19: ## Excluded material\\n20: \\n21: Do not contribute:\\n22: \\n23: - application-specific adapters, schemas, names, paths, or architecture notes;\\n24: - customer, contact, CRM, mailbox, device, or identity data;\\n25: - production endpoints, account IDs, tokens, passwords, or environment files;\\n26: - generated bytecode, caches, database files, traces containing payloads, or\\n27:   local absolute paths; or\\n28: - code copied from a source whose license or contributor authority is unclear.\\n29: \\n30: Synthetic fixtures should be obviously fictional or historical and contain no\\n31: real email address, phone number, account identifier, or source-system key.\\n32: \\n33: ## Contribution provenance\\n34: \\n35: By submitting a contribution, authors must have the right to license it under\\n36: Apache-2.0. Reimplement behavior from public contracts and documentation; do not\\n37: paste private application code and merely rename symbols. If a change adapts\\n38: licensed upstream material, identify the source, version, license, and material\\n39: changes in the pull request and preserve any required notices.\\n40: \\n41: Known-issue reproductions link their public upstream report. Those tests are\\n42: evidence of an upstream lifecycle boundary, not a claim that this provider owns\\n43: or has fixed ActiveGraph itself.\\n44: \\n45: ## Pre-publication check\\n46: \\n47: Before every public commit or release:\\n48: \\n49: 1. inspect the exact staged diff, including binary and untracked files;\\n50: 2. scan source, docs, examples, fixtures, and built artifacts for secrets,\\n51:    personal data, application names, and local paths;\\n52: 3. confirm generated files and `.env` remain ignored;\\n53: 4. verify license headers/notices for any adapted material;\\n54: 5. build the sdist and wheel and inspect their complete file lists; and\\n55: 6. record the commit and test evidence without embedding private payloads.\"}],\"metadata\":{\"truncated\":false}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
{"id":"int-cea840341150b58d6be7b4ee17025342","kind":"tool_call","created_at":"2026-10-13T22:39:02.029Z","actor":"opencode","tool_name":"shell","extra":{"call_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a179b767c5f7d2f2","duration_ms":132,"input":"{\"command\":\"head -30 docs/adr/0002-version-and-transport-support.md; echo '====ADR4===='; head -25 docs/adr/0004-projection-order.md; echo '====ADR1===='; head -15 docs/adr/0001-provider-boundaries.md\"}","message_id":"msg_1376b4639001jO4o4qsOcM70Cs","result":"{\"output\":{\"exit\":0,\"truncated\":false,\"output\":\"# ADR 0002: Stable-first SurrealDB and WebSocket-only support\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-08-30\\n\\n## Decision\\n\\nThe production-qualification lane targets:\\n\\n- SurrealDB server `3.2.4` (current stable on 2026-08-30)\\n- SurrealDB Python SDK `2.0.x`\\n- Python `3.11+`, matching ActiveGraph v1.12\\n- remote WebSocket (`ws://` for the required local qualification lane)\\n- a server process backed by RocksDB\\n\\nSurrealDB `3.3.0-beta.3` is a preview lane, not the stable baseline. Its\\n`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` behavior may be tested but is not required by this\\nadapter because every append already writes the per-run head record.\\n\\n## Why WebSocket only\\n\\nThe Python SDK's stateful transaction handle is WebSocket-only. Although a\\nmulti-statement SurrealQL transaction can be submitted as one query through\\nother transports, this preview rejects HTTP and embedded modes rather than\\nclaim equivalent crash and session semantics without repeatable proof.\\n\\n## Storage engines\\n\\n- Server-side RocksDB is the recommended single-node durable path.\\n- SurrealKV remains beta and is not a production recommendation here.\\n====ADR4====\\n# ADR 0004: Return projected objects in the reference store's insertion order\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-10-12\\n- Decision owners: TrendpilotAI\\n- Upstream baseline: ActiveGraph `v1.12.0` (`50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c`)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nActiveGraph's `GraphStore.all_objects` contract says only that it returns every\\nobject and that \\\"order is unspecified\\\". The reference `InMemoryGraphStore`\\nhappens to return objects in insertion order, and the runtime leans on that\\nobservable behavior in practice: object IDs come from one monotonic per-graph\\ncounter (`task#1`, `claim#2`, ...), and code such as the published pack\\nfixtures treats `graph.objects(type=...)[-1]` as \\\"the most recently created\\nobject of this type\\\".\\n\\nThe first SurrealDB projection ordered reads by `object_id ASC`. Because the\\nlogical IDs are strings, that is a lexicographic sort: it puts `comm_intent#10`\\nbefore `comm_intent#2`, and it orders across types by type name rather than by\\ncreation. The data was correct, but the returned order was not the order the\\nin-memory reference exposes, so behavior that depended on \\\"the last object\\\"\\ndiverged.\\n\\n## Decision\\n====ADR1====\\n# ADR 0001: Keep the event log authoritative and the graph disposable\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-08-30\\n- Decision owners: TrendpilotAI\\n- Upstream baseline: ActiveGraph `v1.12.0` (`50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c`)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nActiveGraph deliberately separates two jobs:\\n\\n1. the `EventStore` preserves what happened; and\\n2. the `GraphStore` answers questions about the current projected state.\\n\\nSurrealDB can host both jobs, plus application documents and native graph\\n\",\"status\":\"completed\"},\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"# ADR 0002: Stable-first SurrealDB and WebSocket-only support\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-08-30\\n\\n## Decision\\n\\nThe production-qualification lane targets:\\n\\n- SurrealDB server `3.2.4` (current stable on 2026-08-30)\\n- SurrealDB Python SDK `2.0.x`\\n- Python `3.11+`, matching ActiveGraph v1.12\\n- remote WebSocket (`ws://` for the required local qualification lane)\\n- a server process backed by RocksDB\\n\\nSurrealDB `3.3.0-beta.3` is a preview lane, not the stable baseline. Its\\n`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` behavior may be tested but is not required by this\\nadapter because every append already writes the per-run head record.\\n\\n## Why WebSocket only\\n\\nThe Python SDK's stateful transaction handle is WebSocket-only. Although a\\nmulti-statement SurrealQL transaction can be submitted as one query through\\nother transports, this preview rejects HTTP and embedded modes rather than\\nclaim equivalent crash and session semantics without repeatable proof.\\n\\n## Storage engines\\n\\n- Server-side RocksDB is the recommended single-node durable path.\\n- SurrealKV remains beta and is not a production recommendation here.\\n====ADR4====\\n# ADR 0004: Return projected objects in the reference store's insertion order\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-10-12\\n- Decision owners: TrendpilotAI\\n- Upstream baseline: ActiveGraph `v1.12.0` (`50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c`)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nActiveGraph's `GraphStore.all_objects` contract says only that it returns every\\nobject and that \\\"order is unspecified\\\". The reference `InMemoryGraphStore`\\nhappens to return objects in insertion order, and the runtime leans on that\\nobservable behavior in practice: object IDs come from one monotonic per-graph\\ncounter (`task#1`, `claim#2`, ...), and code such as the published pack\\nfixtures treats `graph.objects(type=...)[-1]` as \\\"the most recently created\\nobject of this type\\\".\\n\\nThe first SurrealDB projection ordered reads by `object_id ASC`. Because the\\nlogical IDs are strings, that is a lexicographic sort: it puts `comm_intent#10`\\nbefore `comm_intent#2`, and it orders across types by type name rather than by\\ncreation. The data was correct, but the returned order was not the order the\\nin-memory reference exposes, so behavior that depended on \\\"the last object\\\"\\ndiverged.\\n\\n## Decision\\n====ADR1====\\n# ADR 0001: Keep the event log authoritative and the graph disposable\\n\\n- Status: accepted for the research preview\\n- Date: 2026-08-30\\n- Decision owners: TrendpilotAI\\n- Upstream baseline: ActiveGraph `v1.12.0` (`50d8405923b79e4e823a73a3045bb969436a872c`)\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nActiveGraph deliberately separates two jobs:\\n\\n1. the `EventStore` preserves what happened; and\\n2. the `GraphStore` answers questions about the current projected state.\\n\\nSurrealDB can host both jobs, plus application documents and native graph\\n\"}],\"metadata\":{\"status\":\"completed\",\"truncated\":false,\"exit\":0}}","session_id":"ses_ecce71390ffe11p6yPgte61c3p","status":"completed"}}
